WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.301
Serving a file is an authorization
decision, not just a filesystem operation.

2
00:00:04.381 --> 00:00:08.181
Verify object-level authorization before
serving private files, previews,

3
00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:11.083
exports, reports, attachments, or
generated documents.

4
00:00:11.163 --> 00:00:15.194
Public static files and private downloads
should use separate storage and routing

5
00:00:15.274 --> 00:00:15.792
patterns.

6
00:00:15.872 --> 00:00:18.291
Avoid predictable public URLs for private
files.

7
00:00:18.371 --> 00:00:22.171
A download route should know which
business object is being served and why this

8
00:00:22.251 --> 00:00:23.806
caller is allowed to receive it.

9
00:00:23.886 --> 00:00:25.268
Response behavior matters.

10
00:00:25.348 --> 00:00:28.688
Headers should handle content type,
content disposition, caching,

11
00:00:28.768 --> 00:00:31.417
and sniffing behavior appropriately for
the use case.

12
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:35.240
Avoid reflecting untrusted filenames into
headers, HTML, templates, logs,

13
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.932
or JSON without proper handling.

14
00:00:37.012 --> 00:00:40.870
Error responses should not reveal internal
paths, storage keys, stack traces,

15
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.599
server paths, bucket details, or sensitive
metadata.

16
00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:47.595
Safe responses tell the caller what they
need to know without teaching them about

17
00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:48.654
the storage layout.

18
00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:52.362
Tests should cover allowed files, rejected
paths, boundary enforcement,

19
00:00:52.442 --> 00:00:55.897
unauthorized access, large files, cleanup
paths, failed operations,

20
00:00:55.977 --> 00:00:59.605
archive extraction boundaries, and
concurrent behavior where relevant.

21
00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:02.621
Include tests for traversal and
authorization boundaries,

22
00:01:02.701 --> 00:01:04.544
not just happy-path reads and writes.

23
00:01:04.624 --> 00:01:07.503
Pull request review should ask where files
can be read from,

24
00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:11.211
where files can be written, who can
retrieve them, how limits are enforced,

25
00:01:11.291 --> 00:01:13.191
and what happens when something fails.

26
00:01:13.271 --> 00:01:16.842
Logging should capture useful events such
as file created, read, served,

27
00:01:16.922 --> 00:01:19.571
rejected, deleted, expired, quarantined,
or failed.

28
00:01:19.651 --> 00:01:22.357
Logs should not expose file contents,
secrets, tokens,

29
00:01:22.437 --> 00:01:26.238
full sensitive paths, authorization
headers, cookies, private signed URLs,

30
00:01:26.318 --> 00:01:30.061
storage credentials, internal server
paths, or unnecessary personal data.

31
00:01:30.141 --> 00:01:33.826
Safe file handling should be visible in
tests, logs, dashboards, runbooks,

32
00:01:33.906 --> 00:01:38.052
and incident response, but without turning
operational evidence into a new exposure

33
00:01:38.132 --> 00:01:38.420
path.
